  tournaments), per two-models-staircase-recursion.md. TEST that identity:
    n   SC_n  A000568(n-1)  equal?   2^half  2^half==SC
    3      2             1   False        2        True
    4      2             2    True        4       False
    5      8             4   False       16       False
    6     12            12    True       64       False
    7     88            56   False      512       False
    8    176           456   False     4096       False
  => SC_n == A000568(n-1) for ALL n=3..8?  False
     (holds ONLY at n=4 and n=6; FAILS at n=3,5,7,8 -> the canon
      'SC(n)=A000568(n-1)' claim is a small-n coincidence, REFUTED as a
      general identity. SC_n = A002785(n+1), self-converse iso classes.)

  Test exact bijection candidates for ODD n (where (n-1)/2 is integer):
    n=3: 2^half=2, SC_n=2, labeled-tour(1)=2^C(1,2)=1, iso-tour(1)=A000568(1)=1
          2^half == SC_n? True;  2^half == labeled-tour(1)? False
    n=5: 2^half=16, SC_n=8, labeled-tour(2)=2^C(2,2)=2, iso-tour(2)=A000568(2)=1
          2^half == SC_n? False;  2^half == labeled-tour(2)? False
    n=7: 2^half=512, SC_n=88, labeled-tour(3)=2^C(3,2)=8, iso-tour(3)=A000568(3)=2
          2^half == SC_n? False;  2^half == labeled-tour(3)? False

  CONCLUSION (d): 2^half(n) counts GRID-SYM TILINGS (labeled, base-path-
  fixed phi-self-converse tournaments), NOT SC iso classes. 2^half grows
  as 2^floor((n-1)^2/4) (super-exponential in n), while SC_n grows like
  A000568-style.  2^half != SC_n for all n>=4 (equal only at n=3: 2=2).
  The relationship is the BURNSIDE/fixed-point identity (2^half = #fixed
  points of rho), NOT a clean |SC(n)| <-> tournaments-on-(n-1)/2 bijection.
  The half-tiling does NOT by itself realize the SC-halving bijection of
  open-Q#4: that bijection lives at the ISO-CLASS (S_n-quotient) level,
  whereas 2^half is a LABELED/frame-fixed count. Half-tiling gives the
  correct fixed-point COUNT feeding Burnside, but the explicit class-level
  bijection still requires the S_n quotient (remains the open problem).

DONE.
